# SEC sources

Guardrails: G-03, G-08, G-09, G-12

## Documented sources only (G-03)

- Every SEC endpoint, file, and field used in code must be listed in `docs/SOURCE_SCHEMAS.md`
  with a link to the official SEC documentation it came from.
- Until that document exists and covers the item, do not write code that depends on it.
- Never guess a URL pattern, column name, JSON key, unit, or scale. Verify it, or stop and ask.

```text
BAD : row["borrower_name"]   # column name assumed
GOOD: column name taken from docs/SOURCE_SCHEMAS.md, which cites the SEC source
```

## Source hierarchy

- Structured SEC datasets are a scalable bootstrap, not the final authority.
- The original EDGAR filing is the authoritative evidence for what was disclosed.
- Our calculations are derived analytics and are labeled as derived.
- LLM output is never a source (G-08). Structured ingestion is deterministic code, never an LLM.

## Identifiers (G-09)

- A CIK identifies the SEC filing entity (typically the BDC). Keep it on BDC and filing records.
- Never use a CIK as a borrower, legal-entity, or economic-group key.

## Provenance and access (G-12)

- Store every retrieved artifact immutably with source URL, retrieval time, and checksum.
- Send a declared User-Agent read from configuration; never hard-code contact details.
- Respect SEC fair-access limits: shared rate limiting, backoff on errors, caching.
- Fetch (`registry:fetch`) and load (`registry:load`) stay separate. Load is offline from
  the fetch log and content-addressed store. CI never uses the network.
- Additional submissions pages use only the observed URL
  `https://data.sec.gov/submissions/{files[].name}` (SOURCE_SCHEMAS 7.1). Do not invent
  other URL shapes. Names outside the observed pattern are not fetched.
- Never derive a registrant CIK from an accession-number prefix.
- Do not add an npm HTTP or database package unless Node/`psql`/`COPY` is demonstrably
  impractical; stop and ask first.
